Okay, so we gotta go horizontal.

1680
01:20:40,000 --> 01:20:40,760
Look at that.

1681
01:20:41,620 --> 01:20:42,020
Jebediah.

1682
01:20:44,500 --> 01:20:46,180
We have left the atmosphere once again.

1683
01:20:46,360 --> 01:20:48,620
But will we get to 160,000 meters?

1684
01:20:51,180 --> 01:20:53,980
And more importantly, will we orbit?

1685
01:20:57,040 --> 01:20:58,120
Let's see where we are.

1686
01:20:59,280 --> 01:21:01,320
I'm gonna wait until we're a little higher up.

1687
01:21:01,920 --> 01:21:03,600
Less air drag, all that.

1688
01:21:08,910 --> 01:21:09,930
That's so pretty.

1689
01:21:17,050 --> 01:21:18,670
Where's the space center?

1690
01:21:22,760 --> 01:21:23,860
Is that the one right there?

1691
01:21:24,660 --> 01:21:25,340
Look at it.

1692
01:21:26,120 --> 01:21:27,100
Oh yeah, I can kind of see it.

1693
01:21:28,060 --> 01:21:29,120
We're so far away.

1694
01:21:34,500 --> 01:21:35,540
I'm gonna wait a bit more.

1695
01:21:37,080 --> 01:21:38,760
Let's also rotate this lightly.

1696
01:21:41,020 --> 01:21:42,120
And then we do SAS.

1697
01:21:42,500 --> 01:21:42,740
Boom.

1698
01:21:52,740 --> 01:21:53,600
Okay, are we ready?

1699
01:21:54,520 --> 01:21:55,020
Much fuel?

1700
01:21:55,060 --> 01:21:56,300
Oh, I don't even have that much fuel!

1701
01:21:56,760 --> 01:21:57,960
We cannot go to orbit.

1702
01:21:58,760 --> 01:21:59,820
There's no way we do it.

1703
01:22:01,720 --> 01:22:03,360
Oh, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

1704
01:22:03,640 --> 01:22:04,400
So maybe...

1705
01:22:04,400 --> 01:22:05,060
I have an idea, I have an idea.

1706
01:22:05,580 --> 01:22:07,680
Instead of going to orbit, we're gonna do a...

1707
01:22:07,680 --> 01:22:08,920
an emergency switch up.

1708
01:22:11,200 --> 01:22:14,340
We are gonna try and get to 160,000 meters instead.

1709
01:22:14,560 --> 01:22:16,360
We're gonna disband the idea of orbit.

1710
01:22:17,320 --> 01:22:18,880
You have your orders, Jebediah.

1711
01:22:19,740 --> 01:22:20,140
Yes.

1712
01:22:20,940 --> 01:22:29,920
Now, we're gonna have to point along our vector, prograde, give it all you got.

1713
01:22:37,210 --> 01:22:38,410
Fuck, it's not even doing much.

1714
01:22:39,130 --> 01:22:40,190
Turn it even higher up!

1715
01:22:40,590 --> 01:22:41,330
Emergency burning!

1716
01:22:42,670 --> 01:22:43,850
Burn away from the planet!

1717
01:22:45,570 --> 01:22:46,210
Straight up!

1718
01:22:51,610 --> 01:22:52,510
Surely that's enough.

1719
01:22:52,670 --> 01:22:53,610
We're at 150 now.

1720
01:22:53,710 --> 01:22:54,470
Oh, no problem.

1721
01:22:54,570 --> 01:22:55,290
We are gonna make it.

1722
01:23:01,980 --> 01:23:03,520
And then we have to test the decoupler.

1723
01:23:08,110 --> 01:23:08,510
Ha!

1724
01:23:08,730 --> 01:23:09,430
We made it!

1725
01:23:11,690 --> 01:23:12,310
So now?

1726
01:23:12,510 --> 01:23:13,750
Now we have to do, uh, this?

1727
01:23:16,070 --> 01:23:16,470
Yeah!

1728
01:23:19,130 --> 01:23:20,250
And then we do science!

1729
01:23:30,070 --> 01:23:31,690
We have to have the planet in view, though.

1730
01:23:33,050 --> 01:23:33,770
So pretty.

1731
01:23:35,610 --> 01:23:36,970
Mystery goo containment unit.

1732
01:23:37,070 --> 01:23:38,070
Let's see what's happening to the goo.

1733
01:23:41,320 --> 01:23:42,820
Seems to have clumped into a sphere.

1734
01:23:42,940 --> 01:23:44,200
It also appears to have become brittle.

1735
01:23:44,380 --> 01:23:44,620
Nice.

1736
01:23:45,040 --> 01:23:45,720
Sick science!

1737
01:23:46,480 --> 01:23:47,260
That's a lot.

1738
01:23:48,760 --> 01:23:49,980
Then we also had...

1739
01:23:50,500 --> 01:23:51,340
This one?

1740
01:23:52,140 --> 01:23:53,620
The instrument reads zero!

1741
01:23:54,500 --> 01:23:55,100
Surprising.

1742
01:23:56,900 --> 01:23:58,320
What about the temperature?

1743
01:23:59,160 --> 01:24:00,960
The fact that you're over the highlands...

1744
01:24:00,960 --> 01:24:02,900
Does not change the thermometer's opinion...

1745
01:24:02,900 --> 01:24:04,460
On the coldness of space one bit.

1746
01:24:07,240 --> 01:24:08,260
The highlands?

1747
01:24:11,380 --> 01:24:12,320
Crew report.

1748
01:24:13,520 --> 01:24:15,820
It seems we are very much in space right now.

1749
01:24:15,960 --> 01:24:17,780
The sky seems to be mostly below us.

1750
01:24:18,680 --> 01:24:19,300
Hell yeah.

1751
01:24:23,620 --> 01:24:24,580
Look at that.

1752
01:24:24,880 --> 01:24:26,200
Look at our stage down there!

1753
01:24:26,640 --> 01:24:28,400
That's so cool, dude!

1754
01:24:29,460 --> 01:24:30,740
Oh, man.

1755
01:24:32,700 --> 01:24:34,120
It's so damn pretty.

1756
01:24:36,500 --> 01:24:37,160
Eve.

1757
01:24:37,640 --> 01:24:38,300
Duna.

1758
01:24:38,600 --> 01:24:39,140
Moho.

1759
01:24:40,160 --> 01:24:41,640
That's not a planet.

1760
01:24:43,460 --> 01:24:44,620
So something there.

1761
01:24:44,820 --> 01:24:44,940
No?

1762
01:24:45,180 --> 01:24:45,480
Okay.

1763
01:24:47,180 --> 01:24:48,260
Let's close this.

1764
01:24:52,320 --> 01:24:54,240
Get ready for the next stage of the mission.

1765
01:24:55,600 --> 01:24:56,100
Survival.

1766
01:24:56,820 --> 01:24:58,720
We're gonna be coming in at a steep angle.

1767
01:25:05,260 --> 01:25:07,540
Wait, is this some sort of connection that's being made there?

1768
01:25:07,600 --> 01:25:08,000
Look at this!

1769
01:25:08,940 --> 01:25:10,920
Kerbal Space and Island Airfield.

1770
01:25:12,860 --> 01:25:14,120
It's like we can see it.

1771
01:25:14,220 --> 01:25:15,820
Or maybe it's like a line of sight thing.

1772
01:25:24,440 --> 01:25:26,440
Yeah, we're not gonna orbit this time, but that's okay.

1773
01:25:26,620 --> 01:25:30,700
I feel like we always achieve the last goal in the next mission, you know?

1774
01:25:30,800 --> 01:25:32,500
We never achieve the actual goal.

1775
01:25:32,580 --> 01:25:33,620
It's always the last goal.

1776
01:25:35,160 --> 01:25:36,240
We're always a little short.

1777
01:25:37,480 --> 01:25:39,620
Once again, Jebediah living up to his new name.

1778
01:25:42,200 --> 01:25:43,120
Speed up a bit.

1779
01:25:48,430 --> 01:25:49,790
Look at our stage down there.

1780
01:25:50,010 --> 01:25:51,010
Orbiter 1 debris.

1781
01:25:51,950 --> 01:25:53,050
Floating away from us.

1782
01:25:57,860 --> 01:25:58,660
So nice.

1783
01:26:00,400 --> 01:26:01,680
We can go a little faster.

1784
01:26:03,900 --> 01:26:05,580
I do want to take in the views, you know?

1785
01:26:10,750 --> 01:26:14,070
In fact, can I see anything from in here?

1786
01:26:15,950 --> 01:26:17,110
Let me turn off this.

1787
01:26:17,770 --> 01:26:18,770
Oh, that's it!

1788
01:26:19,530 --> 01:26:21,250
This is the new way to view things.

1789
01:26:22,170 --> 01:26:23,510
We're gonna have to turn the vessel, though.

1790
01:26:32,570 --> 01:26:35,270
This is some of the most fun ever, just doing this.

1791
01:26:38,400 --> 01:26:39,200
Show me the planet.

1792
01:26:39,980 --> 01:26:40,680
Ah, we're...

1793
01:26:40,680 --> 01:26:42,920
Actually, we're aligned in such a way where we can't see the planet.

1794
01:26:42,980 --> 01:26:44,000
The planet is below our feet.

1795
01:26:44,360 --> 01:26:44,720
The moon!

1796
01:26:51,040 --> 01:26:51,860
Okay, get ready.

1797
01:26:52,840 --> 01:26:54,280
We gotta put our game phase...

1798
01:26:54,280 --> 01:26:55,840
Oh, we're gonna land in the darkness.

1799
01:26:58,140 --> 01:26:58,900
That's okay, though.

1800
01:27:04,080 --> 01:27:05,900
Point it along a retrograde.

1801
01:27:10,700 --> 01:27:13,120
I feel like the angle is not good here.

1802
01:27:13,240 --> 01:27:14,600
Like, I would put it more like this.

1803
01:27:16,700 --> 01:27:18,260
This looks more right to me, you know?

1804
01:27:20,340 --> 01:27:21,960
But maybe you should just trust the instruments.

1805
01:27:34,880 --> 01:27:36,200
Oh my god, look at that!

1806
01:27:36,540 --> 01:27:37,380
Whoops, wrong button.

1807
01:27:37,800 --> 01:27:38,860
Look at the planet, man!

1808
01:27:40,760 --> 01:27:41,780
That is so pretty.

1809
01:27:44,500 --> 01:27:44,900
Hoi!

1810
01:27:48,290 --> 01:27:48,930
Here we go.

1811
01:27:49,070 --> 01:27:50,010
We're approaching dense air.

1812
01:27:53,240 --> 01:27:53,900
Okay, I'm gonna...

1813
01:27:53,900 --> 01:27:54,820
I'm gonna trust the instruments.

1814
01:27:54,960 --> 01:27:56,200
I'm gonna point towards prograde.

1815
01:28:00,650 --> 01:28:01,470
Look at the angle!

1816
01:28:01,550 --> 01:28:02,370
It looks wrong, doesn't it?

1817
01:28:02,890 --> 01:28:03,690
Call me crazy.

1818
01:28:06,130 --> 01:28:07,110
This feels more right.

1819
01:28:10,010 --> 01:28:11,630
Oh, now it's switched as well.

1820
01:28:13,750 --> 01:28:14,910
Yeah, that's more like it.

1821
01:28:15,550 --> 01:28:17,010
Oh, we are lighting it up.

1822
01:28:17,090 --> 01:28:17,530
Look at this.

1823
01:28:21,540 --> 01:28:22,500
Oh, shit!

1824
01:28:25,800 --> 01:28:26,280
Yo.

1825
01:28:27,140 --> 01:28:28,200
That's fucking sick.

1826
01:28:29,540 --> 01:28:30,740
I can't see nothing.

1827
01:28:30,980 --> 01:28:32,280
I'm so afraid of hitting a mountain.

1828
01:28:32,860 --> 01:28:33,340
Parachute!

1829
01:28:43,180 --> 01:28:43,660
And?

1830
01:28:46,140 --> 01:28:47,440
I'm gonna do the next parachute now.

1831
01:28:56,640 --> 01:28:57,600
I think we're safe.

1832
01:28:57,820 --> 01:28:58,720
Like, nothing can go wrong now.

1833
01:29:10,080 --> 01:29:11,260
Dude, look at this!

1834
01:29:14,820 --> 01:29:15,980
How nice is that?

1835
01:29:21,330 --> 01:29:22,450
Main chute deployed.

1836
01:29:24,710 --> 01:29:26,270
Jebediah hasn't died yet, by the way.

1837
01:29:30,640 --> 01:29:31,040
Jewel.

1838
01:29:31,280 --> 01:29:33,540
I wonder if that's, like, a planet really far away.

1839
01:29:33,660 --> 01:29:35,060
Or if it's one of the closer ones.

1840
01:29:38,140 --> 01:29:38,540
Minmus.

1841
01:29:40,020 --> 01:29:41,620
That's definitely a planet back there, isn't it?

1842
01:29:41,660 --> 01:29:42,140
Why can't I?

1843
01:29:42,340 --> 01:29:42,760
Oh, Dres.

1844
01:29:43,080 --> 01:29:43,240
Yeah.

1845
01:29:54,880 --> 01:29:56,960
I'm getting comfortable with the controls here.

1846
01:29:57,680 --> 01:29:58,420
Splash down!

1847
01:29:59,540 --> 01:30:00,800
We have splash down.

1848
01:30:02,340 --> 01:30:03,920
And what a trip that was, huh?

1849
01:30:04,060 --> 01:30:06,140
We got science done, and we got one contract.

1850
01:30:07,520 --> 01:30:08,220
Complete it.

1851
01:30:09,360 --> 01:30:09,720
Look!

1852
01:30:09,860 --> 01:30:10,640
It didn't register again.

1853
01:30:10,780 --> 01:30:11,280
Like, I clicked.

1854
01:30:13,100 --> 01:30:17,180
Maybe it needs to, like, focus on the panel, and then when I click again is when it's actually registering.

1855
01:30:18,580 --> 01:30:20,160
We got more science out of that.

1856
01:30:22,940 --> 01:30:26,180
And you guys were questioning me putting hard mode, huh?

1857
01:30:26,540 --> 01:30:28,120
Come on, brother.

1858
01:30:29,020 --> 01:30:30,560
What are you talking about?

1859
01:30:31,540 --> 01:30:32,540
Okay, what's this?

1860
01:30:36,580 --> 01:30:37,460
Science Junior.

1861
01:30:37,600 --> 01:30:38,440
I feel like I need to do this.

1862
01:30:39,060 --> 01:30:40,480
Experiment storage unit?

1863
01:30:41,080 --> 01:30:53,000
After discovering that old snack boxes were also really effective at storing mystery goo and data tapes, Kerbal scientists created the experimental storage unit to help Kerbalnauts bring their valuable science back to Kerbin.

1864
01:30:53,000 --> 01:30:55,120
while avoiding the hazards of re-entry.

1865
01:30:55,300 --> 01:30:57,180
Rough landings and Jeb's piloting skills.

1866
01:30:58,720 --> 01:31:00,340
They're referencing Jeb directly.

1867
01:31:00,340 --> 01:31:01,100
That's kind of funny.

1868
01:31:02,480 --> 01:31:03,860
So wait, what is the point of this?

1869
01:31:04,400 --> 01:31:06,420
Like, you put the science in there and you throw it?

1870
01:31:07,200 --> 01:31:07,680
Wait.

1871
01:31:08,400 --> 01:31:09,440
Is it like...

1872
01:31:09,920 --> 01:31:11,720
I'm actually confused what this is.

1873
01:31:13,400 --> 01:31:14,400
What's the point of this?

1874
01:31:15,920 --> 01:31:17,400
I don't understand the description.

1875
01:31:20,530 --> 01:31:22,950
Am I not bringing the science home even without this?

1876
01:31:23,230 --> 01:31:24,450
That's what I'm doing right now.

1877
01:31:28,060 --> 01:31:32,380
The SC-9001 is a cutting-edge, self-contained laboratory.

1878
01:31:32,860 --> 01:31:35,520
Have you ever wondered what happens to something in zero-G?

1879
01:31:35,780 --> 01:31:38,560
Or have you ever pondered the truly meaningful questions?

1880
01:31:38,940 --> 01:31:44,120
Such as, what would happen if I exposed this to the rigors of stellar radiation, an unforgiving vacuum?

1881
01:31:45,300 --> 01:31:47,260
Comes with a complimentary notepad.

1882
01:31:47,560 --> 01:31:49,160
Recommended for ages four to eight.

1883
01:31:49,460 --> 01:31:52,020
Small parts inside make it not suitable for small children.

1884
01:31:52,020 --> 01:31:55,380
I wonder how long kerbals live.

1885
01:31:55,600 --> 01:31:58,940
Because obviously four to eight is a child in human years.

1886
01:31:59,120 --> 01:32:01,540
But maybe for kerbals, it's like...

1887
01:32:01,540 --> 01:32:02,780
It's adults, right?

1888
01:32:03,120 --> 01:32:04,280
They're adults at that time.

1889
01:32:05,460 --> 01:32:06,260
Not sure.

1890
01:32:08,620 --> 01:32:11,860
The Stay Putnik offers the ultimate solution in crew safety.

1891
01:32:12,120 --> 01:32:21,620
A lightweight sphere equipped with remote receivers and relay control input from the ground to the craft, it carries no crew at all, thus keeping them perfectly safe from all harm.

1892
01:32:21,620 --> 01:32:27,040
This device does require electricity to operate, however, and will cease to function if the power runs out.

1893
01:32:27,380 --> 01:32:30,300
The built-in battery should keep it going for about ten minutes.

1894
01:32:31,340 --> 01:32:33,040
Okay, but I guess you can add more batteries then.

1895
01:32:34,460 --> 01:32:35,080
That's cool.

1896
01:32:35,160 --> 01:32:38,020
That's like a way to test missions without putting anyone in danger.

1897
01:32:38,840 --> 01:32:40,300
I really like that!

1898
01:32:41,680 --> 01:32:43,000
Maybe I'll have to do that now.

1899
01:32:43,820 --> 01:32:45,260
Or I save up for this.

1900
01:32:48,320 --> 01:32:51,180
More engines and more fuel tanks could always be good, right?

1901
01:32:56,240 --> 01:32:58,940
Ah, but let's follow the plan I had, which was...

1902
01:32:58,940 --> 01:33:01,120
Oh, I can't even get this anyway, never mind.

1903
01:33:02,120 --> 01:33:03,040
I don't have enough?

1904
01:33:03,640 --> 01:33:04,300
Oh, shit!

1905
01:33:04,560 --> 01:33:05,540
I thought I had enough.

1906
01:33:05,660 --> 01:33:06,480
Okay, I guess I don't.

1907
01:33:07,200 --> 01:33:08,720
Well, that puts a stop to that plan.

1908
01:33:09,800 --> 01:33:11,400
Let's see the contracts.

1909
01:33:15,960 --> 01:33:17,020
Test heat shield.

1910
01:33:19,780 --> 01:33:22,380
Test a radial decoupler on an escape trajectory.

1911
01:33:22,540 --> 01:33:23,640
Oh, that's difficult, though.

1912
01:33:24,680 --> 01:33:28,000
That requires a lot of delta V, and we can't do that right now.

1913
01:33:28,920 --> 01:33:29,520
So, you know what?

1914
01:33:30,320 --> 01:33:31,940
Let's just go to orbit.

1915
01:33:32,080 --> 01:33:33,940
This is like the new primary goal.

1916
01:33:34,060 --> 01:33:34,960
It's just go to orbit.

1917
01:33:37,800 --> 01:33:39,760
It's still orbiter one, because we haven't made it yet.

1918
01:33:39,900 --> 01:33:40,900
So, it's still the same rocket.

1919
01:33:41,020 --> 01:33:42,540
We're just going to adjust it a little bit now.

1920
01:33:44,740 --> 01:33:45,900
Okay, so what is the issue?

1921
01:33:47,640 --> 01:33:50,040
I think the issue is...

1922
01:33:51,700 --> 01:33:52,540
Let me think.

1923
01:33:54,720 --> 01:33:56,420
I think we need more thrust.

1924
01:33:57,840 --> 01:33:59,220
I'm going to do something crazy.

1925
01:34:00,560 --> 01:34:01,460
Check this out.

1926
01:34:01,740 --> 01:34:02,940
We're going to take...

1927
01:34:03,820 --> 01:34:05,180
two of these...

1928
01:34:07,930 --> 01:34:09,170
and simply do that.

1929
01:34:09,870 --> 01:34:11,250
Holy fucking shit, bro.

1930
01:34:13,970 --> 01:34:15,290
4,200 delta V.

1931
01:34:19,760 --> 01:34:21,000
Thrust weight ratio of two.

1932
01:34:21,160 --> 01:34:21,600
That's perfect.

1933
01:34:22,780 --> 01:34:24,060
Let me move these down a bit.

1934
01:34:26,100 --> 01:34:27,120
So that they're aligned.

1935
01:34:27,400 --> 01:34:28,720
It needs to look nice, right?

1936
01:34:30,380 --> 01:34:31,400
I don't like this, though.

1937
01:34:31,540 --> 01:34:34,100
Like, isn't that kind of bad aerodynamically?

1938
01:34:35,180 --> 01:34:37,080
Maybe we can add something to that.

1939
01:34:41,630 --> 01:34:42,410
Wait, we can...

1940
01:34:42,410 --> 01:34:43,790
We can bring more people!

1941
01:34:44,970 --> 01:34:45,630
Oh, wait a minute.

1942
01:34:45,770 --> 01:34:46,230
We're going to...

1943
01:34:46,690 --> 01:34:47,810
We're going to discard them.

1944
01:34:47,990 --> 01:34:48,810
If we do this.

1945
01:34:50,490 --> 01:34:52,510
